### How it was played (same method as build-1)

1. **Deterministic stepping** (`?manual`, `SMB.step(n)`, `SMB.Input.set()`), via `tools/smb_drive.py`: feel measurements, mechanic probes, the complete run.
2. **Real time** (`tools/realtime_check.py`, `tools/probe_mute_music.py`): normal page, rAF loop, real Playwright keyboard events, music elements playing, video recorded.
3. **Complete run**: a seeded bot plays 1-1 from the title with keyboard inputs only. build-1's input log does not replay on build-2 (jump physics changed, which is the D1 fix), so the bot was run again (`tools/bot_search.py`, seed 2009): flag at logic frame 1893 on one life, clear screen at frame 2192, final score 018900. The log `playtests/build-2/win_1-1_seed2009.json` replays it exactly, in Chromium **and** in Firefox (trace, events and sound-call list identical; four screenshots compared pixel by pixel — start, flag slide, tally, clear screen — 0 different pixels).

### What I could not do

- **I did not hear anything.** Sound is judged from which `SMB.Sound` calls fire per event and from decoding the 30 audio files. "Sounds right" is **not verified**.
- **Firefox audio is not verifiable here**: the container has no audio output device, so Firefox logs `NS_ERROR_DOM_MEDIA_MEDIASINK_ERR` for the music files and reports `error.code 3` on the music element (the jingle `ended` event then fires early: hurry → fast music after 0.8 s instead of 2.8 s). I treat that as an environment limit, not a game defect, and did not test Firefox sound further.
- Input latency as a human feels it is not measured (logic frames only). No display, no gamepad.
- Comparison with the original 1-1 is from my memory of the NES game (no reference supplied); marked *assumed*.

### Instrumentation (so nobody mistakes these for normal play)

Same as build-1: arena cleared in `measure_feel.py`/`probes_enemies.py`; teleports, set time, spawned enemies in `probes_*.py`, `probe_pipe4.py`, `probe_mute_music.py` (time set to 102 and Mario placed on the pipe). The complete run (section 3 rows 23, 29), the Firefox replay and the real-time check use **no** teleport or state edit, except that `realtime_check.py`'s hurry-up part sets the time to 102.

## 2. Experience in one paragraph

Build-2 opens straight from `index.html` and plays title → 1-1 → flagpole → castle → clear screen on one life. It is build-1 with the standing-jump fix and a clean console: small Mario now reaches the top of all four pipes from a standstill (apex 66 px against a 64 px pipe), the console shows no failed requests and no page errors, and the title no longer has the hint over the brick row (the controls now show on the "WORLD 1-1 / × 3" screen). Everything else I measured in build-1 behaves the same (speeds, skid, enemies, items, pipe, flag, death, game over, time-up, audio files). I found **no blocker and no major**. One new minor: if you mute with M and the music changes while muted (hurry-up, star, pipe), un-muting leaves the level silent until the next music change.

