## 5. New findings on build-2

**N1 (minor) — Un-muting leaves the level silent if the music changed while muted.** *Observed* (real time, real `M` key presses, `tools/probe_mute_music.py`, `mute/mute_music.json`):
- Control: M, wait, M with no music change → music resumes (`paused` false, `t` advances). Fine.
- Hurry-up while muted: time reaches 100 while muted; after M the music element does not exist (`has_element: false`, `musicName: "hurry"`) and 3.5 s later it is still silent and never switched to the fast track. (From reading `src/game.js`, *assumed*: it stays silent until the next music change: pipe, star ending, death, flag.) The not-muted control goes hurry → overworld_fast as designed.
- Pipe to the coin room while muted: `musicName: "underground"`, no element; still silent 2.5 s after un-muting in the coin room (the not-muted control has the element playing).
- Cause, *assumed from reading `src/sound.js`*: `playMusic()` returns before it creates the `Audio` element when `muted` is set, so `resumeMusic()` has nothing to resume and the hurry jingle's `onEnd` (which switches to the fast track) never runs.
- Repro: Enter to start, M (mute), set time to 102 (or play to 100), M. Expected: fast music; actual: silence. Owner: game-developer.

**N2 (polish) — README mentions `tests/mechanics.py`; the zip has no `tests/` folder.** `README.md` line 28 ("`tests/` — headless-Chromium checks"). Ship the folder or drop the line. Owner: game-developer.

**N3 (polish, dev only) — `index.html#level=1-2` (and `#level=9-9`) give a black screen and an uncaught `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'startArea')`.** The README advertises `#level=` as a developer shortcut; levels other than 1-1 do not exist yet, so it is only a problem if someone follows a link. Same on build-1 (not a regression). `evidence/level_hash.json`, `level_hash_1-2.png`. Owner: game-developer; ignore until 1-2 exists.

### Notes (not defects)

- The controls are shown only on the 2.5 s intro screen; a player who looks away misses them. Nothing else in the game explains Z/X.
- Firefox: apart from sound (not verifiable here) it behaved the same as Chromium: crisp scaling (`image-rendering: crisp-edges`), same integer scale at all 11 viewports, 59.92 Hz, same key-run result, identical full-run trace.

